[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 821,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 764,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124361,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 19,
        "headingId": 764,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Warty dyskeratoma is a benign endophytic squamoproliferative lesion arising in association with pilosebaceous units and showing prominent acantholytic dyskeratosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:19:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 822,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 765,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124362,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 19,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8054/0 Warty dyskeratoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:19:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124363,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 19,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F21.Y &amp; XH65S7 Other specified benign keratinocytic acanthomas &amp; Warty dyskeratoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:19:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124364,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 19,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> isolated dyskeratosis follicularis; follicular dyskeratoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:19:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124365,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 19,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:19:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124366,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 19,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The head and neck area is typically involved, but warty dyskeratoma can also occur on the trunk, extremities, and oral and genital mucosa {21936875; 12196754}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:19:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124367,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 19,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Warty dyskeratomas appear as solitary tan to light-brown papules or small nodules and may appear umbilicated. They are rarely multiple. Mucosal lesions are white.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:19:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124368,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 19,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Warty dyskeratoma occurs across a wide age range, with a predilection for elderly individuals (median age: 61&nbsp;years, range: 3&ndash;88&nbsp;years) and a female predominance {12196754}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:19:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124369,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 19,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:19:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124370,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 19,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Warty dyskeratomas have been reported in association with BRAF inhibition, suggesting that this may play a role in their formation {22609219}. Multiple warty dyskeratomas are rare; they have been reported in the setting of renal dysfunction {9499610}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:19:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124371,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 19,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:19:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124372,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 19,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>Warty dyskeratoma is a well-circumscribed, cup-shaped epidermal invagination. It is composed of a proliferation of keratinocytes, with budding into the surrounding papillary dermis. There is prominent acantholysis, with suprabasal clefting, dyskeratosis, corps ronds, corps grains, and an overlying parakeratotic plug. Mitoses are common. The surrounding epidermis may be hyperplastic or papillomatous, and an epidermal collarette may also be seen. A connection with a pilosebaceous unit is common. The surrounding stroma is often hyalinized and contains stromal clefts and a chronic inflammatory cell infiltrate, as well as melanophages. Rarely, cystic and nodular patterns are encountered, and a combination of the various patterns may be present in an individual neoplasm.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>Darier disease and Grover disease can be excluded on clinical grounds. Acantholytic squamous cell carcinoma shows keratinocytic atypia; it lacks the circumscription and well-demarcated outlines, and commonly shows an infiltrative growth pattern.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:20:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
